  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 417 AM MDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nThe upper-level high previously centered over the Four Corners has \nbegun to dissolve, and as such, flow aloft will remain zonal and \nweak today, continuing to allow a bit of monsoonal moisture to seep \ninto the Desert Southwest and parts of Colorado. The better \nafternoon shower coverage will reside over the high terrain. \nHowever, with model soundings over the Denver metro and eastern CO\nplains showing inverted-V soundings and DCAPE values ~1200 J/kg, \nvirga showers will still be able to produce strong outflow winds \nup to 40 mph even with little precipitation reaching the surface.\nWe expect afternoon highs to reach the mid 90s over the Denver \nmetro, with a few 100s possible over the eastern Colorado plains. \nIf increasing moisture over the region Tuesday evening is able to \nkeep clouds around through most of the night, morning lows on \nWednesday may only reach the mid 60s across the low elevations. \n\nMonsoonal moisture transport ramps up on Wednesday as the upper \nlevel ridge redevelops to our east with the accompanying high \ncentered over the southern U.S. plains. This pattern change will \nmoderate afternoon highs to near-normal on Wednesday, and much \ncooler than normal Thursday through Saturday (low 80s across the low \nelevations). Additionally, an upper level trough will begin to take \nshape just off the coast of California. From a synoptic standpoint, \nthe combination of the trough off the Pacific coast and the ridge \nover the south-central Plains will help channel Pacific moisture \ninto the Four Corners and provide a classic monsoonal setup. \nShower coverage will become widespread on Wednesday starting over\nwestern Colorado in the late morning and spreading over the Front\nRange, foothills, and eventually the Denver metro and eastern \nColorado as the day progresses. Locally heavy rainfall may also \noccur with the strongest showers and storms. Precipitable water \n(PW) values on Wednesday will rise to around the 90th percentile \nof climatology (0.97\"). However, shallow warm cloud layers and \nstorm motion vectors ~20KT will limit flash flooding potential.\n\nMoisture is projected to peak on Thursday afternoon as mid level \nPacific moisture and low level Gulf moisture give way to PW values \nthat are 150%-200% of the climatological average (~1.4\"). From a \nflash flood mesoanalysis perspective, a relatively deep warm \ncloud layer (~5,000 ft), slower storm motions (LCL-EL mean wind \n~15KT), and a long-skinny CAPE profile will lead to conditions \nthat are favorable the production of efficient rainfall. If this\nforecast trend continues, flood watches for flash flooding \nconcerns may need to be issued in the next 48 hours, once the \nfootprint of the heaviest precipitation becomes more clear. \nAdditionally, if the rainfall on Wednesday overperforms, areas \nwith increased soil moisture may be more prone to flash flooding\non Thursday.\n\nFor Friday, PW values will still greatly exceed the 90th percentile \nof climatology and a shortwave trough may provide additional forcing \nfor showers and storms. Faster storm motions will limit flash \nflooding potential unless heavy rainfall occurs over areas with near-\nsaturated soils from the previous day's rain. Long-range model \nensemble members suggest a slight decrease in moisture for the \nweekend, although most solutions still show PWs in the 1.0-1.2\" \nrange due to continued southwest flow aloft. \n\nWe are fairly confident that there will be daily chances for \nprecipitation each afternoon through at least early next week.\nEnsemble spread does suggest uncertainty entering the picture \nhalfway through next week, although near-average monsoonal \nmoisture still favors afternoon showers over the high terrain on \nmost days. A warming temperature trend will begin on Monday, with \n90-degree afternoon highs over the Denver metro becoming likely \nlate next week.",
